2,2,2,8,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,8,2,2,2,8],[8,9,9,9,8,9,9,9,8,9,9,9,8,9,9,9,8,9,9,9,8,9,9,9,8,9,9,9,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## What went wrong\n\nThe program changed nothing because `panels` returned one box, the whole grid. It found no divider lines because they span the full grid edge-to-edge, including the border. That is likely why the helper ignores them. The tile analysis never ran. The rule itself is unaffected. Only the lattice detection needs replacing, and I can do it directly. The divider colour is the colour of the top-left corner. A row (or column) is a divider when every cell in it has that colour. The tiles are the gaps between consecutive dividers.\n\n## The rule\n\n1. **Lattice.** The grid is a 7×7 array of 3×3 tiles separated by single-colour lines. Most tiles repeat one \"normal\" pattern. A few tiles share a different \"special\" pattern.\n2. **Shape mask.** The special tile uses a colour the normal tile lacks. The cells of that colour form a 3×3 shape:\n   - Pair 1: colour 3 forms a U.\n   - Pair 2: colour 1 forms an H.\n   - Pair 3: colour 8 forms `.X./XXX/X.X`.\n3. **Fractal enlargement.** The shape is drawn again at lattice scale, with one tile per mask cell. It is placed at the unique offset where every existing special tile lands on a mask cell. Then the special pattern is stamped on the remaining mask cells.\n\n**Check against the training pairs** (traced by hand):\n- **Pair 1:** the bar at tile row 3, columns 2–4 is the U's bottom. Tiles are added at rows 1–2, columns 2 and 4. These are exactly the changed cells, y 5–11 at x 9–11 and x 17–19.\n- **Pair 2:** the bar is the H's middle row. Tiles are added at rows 1 and 3, columns 1 and 3.\n- **Pair 3:** the four existing special tiles fit the mask only at offset (2,1). Tiles (2,2) and (4,3) are added, which are y 9–11/x 9–11 and y 17–19/x 13–15.\n\n**Test:** the special tile is `9 4 9 / 4 9 4 / 4 4 4`. The new colour is 4, so the mask is `.X. / X.X / XXX`. The existing special tiles are at (3,2), (4,1) and (4,3). They fit only at offset (3,1), so the program stamps tile row 5, columns 1–3. That completes the bottom bar.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    from collections import Counter\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    dc = g[0, 0]\n    drows = [y for y in range(H) if np.all(g[y] == dc)]\n    dcols = [x for x in range(W) if np.all(g[:, x] == dc)]\n\n    def spans(divs, size):\n        res, prev = [], -1\n        for d in divs + [size]:\n            if d - prev > 1:\n                res.append((prev + 1, d - 1))\n            prev = d\n        return res\n\n    rs, cs = spans(drows, H), spans(dcols, W)\n    cell = {}\n    for i, (y0, y1) in enumerate(rs):\n        for j, (x0, x1) in enumerate(cs):\n            cell[(i, j)] = (y0, x0, y1, x1)\n    n, m = len(rs), len(cs)\n    pats = {k: tuple(map(tuple, g[b[0]:b[2] + 1, b[1]:b[3] + 1])) for k, b in cell.items()}\n    normal = Counter(pats.values()).most_common(1)[0][0]\n    specials = [k for k, p in pats.items() if p != normal]\n    out = g.copy()\n    if not specials:\n        emit(out.copy(), \"No special tiles; nothing to change.\")\n        return out\n    T = np.array(pats[specials[0]])\n    normal_cols = set(np.array(normal).ravel().tolist())\n    newcols = [c for c in set(T.ravel().tolist()) if c not in normal_cols]\n    mask = np.isin(T, newcols)\n    mh, mw = mask.shape\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Lattice of %dx%d tiles; the special tile's new colour %s forms a %dx%d shape mask.\"\n         % (n, m, newcols, mh, mw))\n    sset = set(specials)\n    chosen = None\n    for oy in range(n - mh + 1):\n        for ox in range(m - mw + 1):\n            mc = {(oy + i, ox + j) for i in range(mh) for j in range(mw) if mask[i, j]}\n            if sset <= mc:\n                chosen = mc\n                break\n        if chosen:\n            break\n    if chosen is None:\n        emit(out.copy(), \"No placement found; unchanged.\")\n        return out\n    for (r, c) in sorted(chosen):\n        if (r, c) in cell and (r, c) not in sset:\n            y0, x0, y1, x1 = cell[(r, c)]\n            if (y1 - y0 + 1, x1 - x0 + 1) == T.shape:\n                out[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = T\n                emit(out.copy(), \"Stamp the special tile at tile (%d,%d) to grow the enlarged shape.\" % (r, c))\n    emit(out.copy(), \"Final: the special tiles now form the tile's own shape at lattice scale.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The program changed nothing because `panels` returned one box, the whole grid."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The divider colour is the colour of the top-left corner."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"A row (or column) is a divider when every cell in it has that colour."}],"repeat":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The tile analysis never ran."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Shape mask.** The special tile uses a colour the normal tile lacks."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Fractal enlargement.** The shape is drawn again at lattice scale, with one tile per mask cell."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Then the special pattern is stamped on the remaining mask cells."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"They fit only at offset (3,1), so the program stamps tile row 5, columns 1–3."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamp the special tile at tile (1,2) to grow the enlarged shape."}],"scale":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Fractal enlargement.** The shape is drawn again at lattice scale, with one tile per mask cell."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Final: the special tiles now form the tile's own shape at lattice scale."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"2707b314b3617426def29d62cbd60a11b4524a7ac33751133fb